#
# Residue — Benjamini–Hochberg (P5)
#
# See clr.residue for the record format.  Two modules bear on this file:
#   `CompositionalDA.BenjaminiHochberg`        the per-rank scaling `bhScale`
#   `CompositionalDA.BenjaminiHochberg.StepUp` the running-minimum step-up
#                                              that `src/Core/bh.jl` computes
#
# The archived MetaManifold version of this file recorded "ℚᵘ has no ⊓ lemmas"
# and "q_i ≥ p_i is FALSE in general and must not be attempted".  Both were
# true of what was being modelled there and are false of what is modelled
# here: the stdlib at the pinned SHA has the full `_⊓_` lattice for `ℚᵘ`, and
# `q_i ≥ p_i` is a theorem of the STEP-UP (running minimum from `running = 1`,
# rank ≤ family size, p ∈ [0,1]), which is what the Julia code computes.  It
# remains false of the bare per-rank scaling, where `M/(j+1)` can be < 1.

R-BH-1
  statement: |
    ∀ M j n d → 0ℚᵘ ≤ℚ bhScale M j (+ n) d
    (the scaled value of a non-negative p-value is non-negative, in the
    `bhScale` form with the denominator expanded)
  status: OPEN for `bhScale`; CLOSED for the product form by
    `StepUp.scale-≥` (`0 ≤ p → p ≤ scale m k p`, which with `0 ≤ p` gives
    `0 ≤ scale m k p` by `≤-trans`), under the rank bound `suc k ≤ m`.
  why: |
    `+ M * + n` does not reduce to `+ (M ℕ.* n)`; `ℤ._*_` unfolds to a
    sign-times-absolute-value form, and the non-negativity lemmas in
    `Data.Integer.Properties` are stated about the reduced form.  The StepUp
    module avoids the obstacle by keeping the scaling as a `ℚᵘ` product
    (`p * mkℚᵘ (+ m) k`) and using `*-monoʳ-≤-nonNeg` with a `NonNegative`
    instance built from the `0 ≤ p` hypothesis.
  impact: LOW.  A negative q-value would need a negative p-value, which
    `bh_adjust` refuses with an `ArgumentError`.
  closes-with: |
    `scale-is-bhScale : scale m k (mkℚᵘ n d) ≃ bhScale m k n d`
    (a `*-comm`/`+-comm` rearrangement of the denominator), after which
    R-BH-1 follows from `scale-≥` without the rank bound being needed for
    non-negativity alone.

R-BH-2
  statement: |
    The BH step-up running minimum: adjusted values are ≤ 1, descending in
    rank, ≥ the raw p-value, and monotone (p_i ≤ p_j ⇒ q_i ≤ q_j) including
    across ties.
  status: CLOSED (by `StepUp.adjust-≤1`, `StepUp.adjust-descending`,
    `StepUp.adjust-≥`, `StepUp.adjust-monotone`, and at original indices by
    `StepUp.Unsorted.bh-≤1`, `StepUp.Unsorted.bh-≥`,
    `StepUp.Unsorted.bh-monotone`)
  why: n/a
  impact: n/a
  closes-with: n/a

R-BH-3
  statement: |
    `ord = sortperm(q; rev = true)` returns a permutation π such that the
    sorted sequence is descending (`Linked _≥_ sorted` in
    `StepUp.Unsorted`).
  status: NOT ATTEMPTED (named assumption)
  why: |
    `sortperm` is not re-implemented in Agda.  Every `Unsorted` theorem takes
    `Linked _≥_ sorted` as an explicit hypothesis and so says: IF π sorts
    descending THEN the property holds at the original indices.  That
    Julia's `sortperm` with `rev = true` produces such a π is tested, not
    proved.
  impact: |
    LOW.  `sortperm` is Base Julia and the property test
    `test/property_bh.jl` evaluates the end-to-end result at the original
    indices.
  closes-with: |
    A verified sort (e.g. insertion sort with a `Linked _≥_` output proof and
    a permutation witness) substituted for π.  Not planned.

R-BH-4
  statement: |
    Every p-value handed to the step-up lies in [0, 1].
  status: DISCHARGED BY A JULIA REFUSAL (not proved)
  why: |
    `bh_adjust` raises `ArgumentError` for any finite value outside [0, 1]
    before the loop runs.  The Agda hypotheses `All (0ℚᵘ ≤_)` and
    `All (_≤ 1ℚᵘ)` are exactly what that guard establishes.  The guard
    itself is tested, not proved.
  impact: |
    The hypotheses are load-bearing: without `0 ≤ p` the scaling runs the
    wrong way and `adjust-monotone` fails; without `p ≤ 1` the initial
    `running = 1` undercuts the largest p-value and `adjust-≥` fails.
  closes-with: n/a (a Julia-side guard cannot be proved in Agda).

R-BH-5
  statement: |
    NaN entries are excluded before the step-up and remain NaN afterwards;
    `m` is the count of non-NaN entries.
  status: OUT OF SCOPE
  why: |
    `ℚᵘ` has no NaN.  The Agda model is the loop over the NaN-free vector,
    with `m` its length.  The exclusion and the write-back are Julia code,
    tested by `prop_bh_monotone` (which asserts NaN-in ⇒ NaN-out).
  impact: LOW.
  closes-with: n/a

R-BH-6
  statement: |
    The floating-point `bh_adjust` agrees with the exact `adjust` to within
    the `1e-15` tolerance the property test uses.
  status: OUT OF SCOPE
  why: |
    IEEE-754 semantics are not modelled.  `q[k] * m / rank` in Float64 is a
    rounding of `scale m k p`; the tolerance in `test/properties.jl` is the
    test's allowance for that, not a proved bound.
  impact: MEDIUM in principle; bounded by the property test and the planted
    `mutant_bh_no_cummin`.
  closes-with: A verified floating-point model.  Not planned.

R-BH-7
  statement: |
    The q-values control the false discovery rate at the nominal level.
  status: OUT OF SCOPE
  why: |
    FDR control is a theorem about a probability measure over a sampling
    model.  This development is about exact rational arithmetic.  Nothing in
    `proofs/` bears on it, and `proofs/PROOF-STATUS.md` says so in its own
    words.
  impact: n/a (the claim is not made).
  closes-with: n/a
